                            <a name="comment2865"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Versimilitude
</h4>

<p>
Last year, I had the privilege of participating in small group sessions when Jeffrey Eugenides -- author of Virgin Suicides and Middlesex -- visited U.Michigan and I paid close attention to what he had to say about process and method. It struck me how much of the craft behind his art -- and I do consider novels at that level works of art -- resembled what we hear from the great nonfiction and even newspaper narrative writers.<br />
<br />
It was both comforting and intimidating to hear directly from a writer of his caliber how much rewriting and revising and excising goes into creating great stories.<br />
<br />
From my black moleskine, selected notes:<br />
--said he had written 500 pages of manuscript on Middlesex when he realized, "OK, now I'm at page 1."<br />
--Says he rewrote each chapter 20 times<br />
--Talks about over-research and using an abundance of material (reminded me of longform narrative). Also, the serendipity of how you encounter different things in the research that later will inform the writing<br />
--Researches like a narrative writer, looking for photos that will inform and also steeping himself in music<br />
--&#8220;I think about the reader. I think about keeping the readers attention. I want to provide pleasure for the reader.&#8221;<br />
--&#8220;Wanted it lively, energetic and to be the kind of experience I had reading in my 20s.&#8221;<br />
--Allows little slips and seams of narrative voice to reveal bona fides to reader<br />
--&#8220;The high postmodernists believe narrative is just a device. I don&#8217;t believe that because I believe narrative is elemental.&#8221;<br />
--Some influences include PaleFire b y Nabokov, Midnights Children by Rushdie<br />
--Recommends book about writing: ENEMIES OF PROMISE  by Cyrill Connolly
</p>
