                            <a name="comment4212"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Versimilitude
</h4>

<p>
Seems Preston is quoted as saying:<br />
<br />
"The characters come alive on their own, and they end up showing me where the story must go."<br />
<br />
That would be believable, except maybe if someone has seen in person what he is writing about, and works in a profession related.<br />
<br />
Have you read The Wild Trees?<br />
<br />
Well ... here are the trees:<br />
<br />
http://www.mdvaden.com/grove_of_titans.shtml<br />
<br />
After comparing a couple of the undisclosed groves to Preston's descriptions, I think he lets the characters show him the extreme limits he can lead the story.<br />
<br />
It's factual for the more part - I'll give it that. But it's decribed in such a way as to lead readers into a different perception than what  really exists.<br />
<br />
I still like reading the book. Even recommend it.<br />
<br />
But going there in person, really changed how I view his writing and took a lot of wind out of his sails. Personally, I believe he's interested into building shock and awe factor with facts and the abilities of people.<br />
<br />
Like in the Wild Trees, where he wrote about the death of a forest climber that was totally unrelated to the main story. And I"m not referring to the fall of Hillary in the book, who climbed with Sillett.<br />
<br />
Cheers,<br />
<br />
M. D. Vaden of Oregon<br />
<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="http://www.mdvaden.com/grove_of_titans.shtml">M. D. Vaden of Oregon</a>
	

