                            <a name="comment4215"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Versimilitude
</h4>

<p>
In reference to Bill's post and questions, I'll start with this:<br />
<br />
"No perception can exist outside the mind of the perceiver. If what you're saying is that your perception of the groves is different from Preston's, that's fair but largely irrelevant. If you're saying that Preston's description does not match reality, then how can you say he's "factual"?<br />
This is an example of how sloppy thought leads to sloppy writing."<br />
<br />
I did not say it was factual, I said it was mostly factual. <br />
<br />
To clarify, if Preston writes "bushwhack" to describe entry into a grove that is virtually a stroll or hike, that's stretching it.<br />
<br />
How about if a valley was not hidden, but was referred to as "hidden"? Or was not uncharted, but is called "uncharted"?<br />
<br />
A similar example exists on a video where a research scientist goes one step further as to add the word "lost" for "lost hidden valley", when in reality (non-fiction world) no valley in Redwood Creek National Park is lost. That's where Hyperion is, the tallest known redwood, included in Preston's book.<br />
<br />
I can't explain too much detail, otherwise I'd be disclosing the location of the groves.<br />
<br />
But I'm glad Bill brought up the point about "perception". And it appears that no debate is required here, except for adding more clarification for information I probably should have included earlier.<br />
<br />
Thanks.<br />
<br />
<br />

</p>
